{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":2,"query":"name=Magee&limit=10&title=Confessions of a Philosopher","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Confessions of a philosopher","subTitle":"a journey through Western philosophy"},"name":[{"@nameTitleGroup":"1","@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Magee, Bryan","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Quine, W. V. (Willard Van Orman)","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"former owner."}}}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Introductions"},{"@authority":"rbprov","#text":"Association copies (Provenance)"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York"}}],"publisher":"Random House","dateIssued":["c1997",{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"1997"}],"edition":"1st U.S. ed.","issuance":"monographic"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"x, 480 p. ; 25 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"In this book, Magee tells the story of his own discovery of philosophy and not only makes it come alive but shows its relevance to daily life. Magee is the Carl Sagan of philosophy, the great popularizer of the subject, and author of a major new introductory history, The Story of Philosophy. This book follows the course of Magee's life, exploring philosophers and ideas as he himself encountered them, introducing all the great figures and their ideas, from the pre-Socratics to Bertrand Russell and Karl Popper, including Wittgenstein, Kant, Nietzsche, and Schopenhauer, rationalism, utilitarianism, empiricism, and existentialism.--From publisher description."},"tableOfContents":"Scenes from childhood -- My introduction to academic philosophy -- Logical positivism and its refutation -- Linguistic analysis -- The inadequacy of linquistic philosophy -- The problem of perception -- What can be shown but not said -- A Yale education -- The discovery of Kant -- Professional versus amateur philosophy -- Getting to know Popper -- Getting to know Russell -- First attempts at a political philosophy -- The search for meaning -- Mid-life crisis -- A philosophical novel -- Groves of academe -- In praise of popularization -- The limits of philosophy -- The discovery of Schopenhauer -- The philosophy of Schopenhauer -- Philosophy on television -- The main split in contemporary philosophy -- Left wondering.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Bryan Magee."},"\"This work was originally published in Great Britain by Weidenfeld & Nicolson ... in 1997\"--T.p. verso.","Includes index.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-uk---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Magee, Bryan"}},{"@authority":"rvm","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Magee, Bryan"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Philosophy","genre":"Introductions"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Philosophers","geographic":"Great Britain","genre":"Biography"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"B1647.M186 A3 1998"},{"@edition":"21","@authority":"ddc","#text":"192"},{"@edition":"21","@authority":"ddc","#text":"B"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Confessions of a philosopher"},"name":{"namePart":"Magee, Bryan."},"originInfo":{"publisher":"New York : Random House, ©1997","edition":"1st U.S. ed."},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)605532915"